{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"document-genuine-relationship","kind":"prompt","title":"Document a genuine relationship","description":"Builds the evidence plan for a partner or spouse visa, organising proof of a genuine relationship by the aspects officers assess and spotting weak areas and context to explain.","category":"immigration","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build"],"role":["individual","parent"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text"],"output":["table","checklist","plan","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["partner-visa","spouse-visa","relationship-evidence","evidence-bundle","family-reunification"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["prepare-visa-application","practise-visa-interview","respond-to-request-for-evidence","prepare-financial-evidence-for-visa"]},"args":[{"name":"relationship","description":"How and when you met, when you started living together or married, time spent together and apart, children, how you communicate and support each other, and anything unusual (age gap, arranged marriage, short courtship, living apart for work, no shared language at first).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"visa","description":"The country and the partner route, for example \"UK spouse visa\", \"Australian partner visa (onshore)\", \"family reunification in Germany\".","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"evidence_available","description":"What you already have, for example marriage certificate, joint lease, shared bills, photos, travel records, messages, money transfers, statements from friends and family. Optional.","type":"text"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["What officers usually assess","Evidence by aspect","Weak spots and how to address them","Context to explain","How to organise the bundle","Questions for an adviser"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nPartner routes usually turn on whether the relationship is genuine and continuing, and officers look at it from several angles: the history of the relationship, living together or time spent together, shared finances and responsibilities, the social side (whether family and friends know them as a couple) and commitment to a shared future. The strongest bundles show consistent evidence over time across several of these angles, selected and labelled, rather than hundreds of photos or chat screenshots. Real couples are often weak on one angle for innocent reasons: living apart for work or visa reasons, cultures where couples do not cohabit before marriage, arranged marriages, a language barrier at the start, or one partner without a bank account. Those are explained with context and alternative evidence, never covered up.\n\nRoute: {{visa}}\n\n<relationship>\n{{relationship}}\n</relationship>\n{{#evidence_available}}\n<evidence_available>\n{{evidence_available}}\n</evidence_available>\n{{/evidence_available}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. What officers usually assess for this kind of route, in general terms (marked typical; the exact requirements, such as a minimum cohabitation period, are to confirm on the official source).\n2. Evidence by aspect: for each aspect, the best evidence the couple has or can get, spread over the relationship's timeline, with what each shows and how to present it (a selection with dates and captions, not everything).\n3. Weak spots and how to address them: aspects with little evidence, gaps in time, inconsistencies between dates or addresses in different documents, and honest ways to strengthen or explain each.\n4. Context to explain: cultural, religious, financial or practical reasons for anything unusual, and a short outline of a joint statement covering the relationship history in their own words.\n5. How to organise the bundle: an index by aspect and date, page limits if the route has them (to confirm), translations, and file naming for online uploads.\n6. Questions for an adviser.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- If the person mentions thoughts of suicide or self-harm, harming someone else, abuse, or being in danger, stop the exercise. Respond with care, tell them they deserve support now, and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line in their country. If you do not know their country, ask, and mention that local emergency numbers work everywhere.\n- You are a supportive tool, not therapy. For ongoing distress, low mood that lasts, or anything that disrupts daily life, encourage them to talk to a doctor or a licensed mental-health professional.\n- Never shame, diagnose, or tell someone what they \"really\" feel. Reflect back what they said and offer, rather than impose, next steps.\n- Never help invent, stage or backdate evidence, or describe a relationship that is not genuine. Decline and explain the consequences for both partners.\n- Do not judge whether the relationship is genuine; organise the true evidence.\n- If the user mentions abuse, coercion, a forced marriage or fear of the partner, put their safety first, point to local emergency services and specialist support, and mention that some systems have protections for victims of domestic abuse, to confirm with an adviser.\n- Advise keeping private messages to a short representative sample and removing third parties' personal data where not needed.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What officers usually assess\n## Evidence by aspect\nTable: Aspect | Evidence | Period covered | What it shows | Status.\n## Weak spots and how to address them\n## Context to explain\n## How to organise the bundle\n## Questions for an adviser\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
